I have a few ultimate identity keys stored in hardware, tamper-resistant
storage (such as Yubikey). The private material never leaves there.

Hardware storage may be lost or broken, that's why I have a few of them, and
newer ones will be certified by older ones, before older ones break.

I also have a daily driver key, certified by all ultimate identity keys.
This daily driver key could be stolen, since it stays across different machines
I have used. If I found it has been, then I will revoke it. If you are
suspicious, feel free to request a signature from one of my ultimate keys.

Please be aware that advance malware can trick my machine to request my
hardware storage to sign for something, when it's plugged in my computer.
There's little defense to that, except to minimize such exposure windows and to
require PIN upon every signature.

Current keys:
pk.gpg (oldest hardware ultimate key)
yubi2-pub.gpg (2nd hardware ultimate key)
daily-pub.gpg (daily driver key).
